      "claim": "Oral ATRA, the closest retinoid acid analog, has an estimated oral bioavailability of 25-30% in healthy volunteers, which is above the 20% threshold.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C46"],
      "claim": "Isotretinoin, the 13-cis retinoid acid analog, has a systemic bioavailability of about 40%, supporting adequate oral absorption for retinoid-like molecules.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C08"],
      "claim": "Acitretin, a structurally related retinoid, has an absolute oral bioavailability of approximately 60% (range 36-95%), well above the 20% threshold.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C25"],
      "claim": "Etretinate, the ethyl ester analog, has an absolute oral bioavailability of about 40% (range 30-70%), supporting high bioavailability for lipophilic retinoids.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C23"],
      "claim": "Paracetamol, which shares the 4-hydroxyanilide substructure, has an absolute oral bioavailability of 63%, indicating that this amide/phenol motif is compatible with good absorption.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The query is a retinoid amide (fenretinide-like). The closest retinoid acid/ester analogs consistently show oral bioavailability values of 25-72%, all above the 20% threshold. The 4-hydroxyanilide substructure is also associated with good absorption in paracetamol. No affirmative quantitative evidence supports F < 20%.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement for fenretinide itself is provided.",
    "The effect of the amide bond and phenolic hydroxyl on intestinal permeability and first-pass metabolism is inferred from analogs rather than directly measured.",
